Title:
Computer Architecture for Reverse Engineers and Malware Developers

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post is a reverse-engineer-oriented primer on how x86-64 systems actually execute code, focusing on memory hierarchy, virtual memory, CPU execution, registers, the call stack, calling conventions, and Windows x64 stack unwinding.  
- It explains why performance and behavior depend heavily on caches (locality, cache lines, coherence) and why DRAM/SSD characteristics matter for real-world execution and analysis.  
- The virtual memory section ties page tables, permissions (NX/RW), TLBs, and page faults to practical security implications like DEP/NX, ASLR, W^X, injection mechanics, and guard pages.  
- It maps disassembly patterns to ABI rules (Windows x64 vs SysV), showing how arguments/returns move through registers/stack and why patterns like `sub rsp, ...` and shadow space exist.  
- The unwinding section details how Windows x64 uses `.pdata/.xdata` unwind metadata (RUNTIME_FUNCTION/UNWIND_INFO) rather than frame-pointer chains, and why this matters for stack walking and call stack spoofing detection/abuse.  
- Useful for reverse engineers, exploit developers, malware developers, and defenders building stack-walk-based detections or analyzing evasions.

Technical Focus:
- Memory hierarchy (registers/L1-L3/DRAM), cache lines, locality, MESI coherence, inclusivity
- DRAM internals (refresh, destructive reads, row activation/precharge) and physical address space/MMIO
- Virtual memory (page tables, CR3, MMU/TLB, page faults, permission bits NX/RW)
- x86-64 registers and ABI calling conventions (Windows x64 shadow space vs SysV red zone)
- Stack frames, prologues/epilogues, and Windows x64 unwind metadata / RtlVirtualUnwind

Use Cases:
- Interpreting x86-64 disassembly by recognizing ABI-driven register/stack patterns
- Designing/debugging shellcode loaders and injection flows (VirtualAlloc/VirtualProtect, remote memory writes) with correct calling convention and alignment
- Understanding and evading stack-walk-based detections (call stack spoofing concepts, unwind-table constraints)
- Analyzing crash dumps/exceptions via page fault mechanics and unwind behavior
- Reasoning about cache effects relevant to side channels and self-modifying/unpacked code execution

Keywords:
x86-64, memory hierarchy, DRAM, SRAM, CPU cache, cache line, locality, MESI, cache coherence, inclusive cache, virtual memory, page tables, CR3, MMU, TLB, page fault, NX bit, DEP, ASLR, W^X, VirtualAlloc, VirtualProtect, mprotect, calling convention, Windows x64 ABI, SysV AMD64, shadow space, red zone, RSP alignment, prologue, epilogue, stack frame, stack unwinding, .pdata, .xdata, RUNTIME_FUNCTION, UNWIND_INFO, RtlVirtualUnwind, call stack spoofing, direct syscalls, TEB, PEB